[[File:Maradona-Mundial 86 con la copa.JPG|thumb|upright|[[Diego Maradona]] celebrating with the [[FIFA World Cup trophy|Trophy]]. [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] won the tournament unbeaten]]
The '''1986 FIFA World Cup''' was the 13th [[FIFA World Cup]], the quadrennial [[Association football|football]] tournament for men's senior national teams. It was played in Mexico from 31 May to 29 June 1986. The tournament was the second to feature a 24-team format. Colombia had been originally chosen to host the competition by [[FIFA]] but, largely due to economic reasons, was not able to do so, and withdrew in November 1982. Mexico was selected as the new host in May 1983, and became the first country to host the World Cup more than once, after previously [[1970 FIFA World Cup|hosting the 1970 edition]]. The World Cup was won by [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] (their second title, after winning in [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]]). Argentina was captained by the 25-year-old [[Diego Maradona]], who played a large part in his team's success by scoring his "[[The hand of God|Hand of God]]" goal, as well as another voted the "[[Argentina v England (1986 FIFA World Cup)#The Goal of the Century|Goal of the Century]]", in the [[Argentina v England (1986 FIFA World Cup quarter-final)|same quarter-final]] match against England. These were two of the five goals that Maradona scored during the tournament, and he also created another five for his teammates.<ref name="Over">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=502188/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140709171155/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=502188/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=9 July 2014 |title=1986 FIFA World Cup Mexico – Overview |work=FIFA.com |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |access-date=11 March 2013}}</ref> Argentina beat [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] 3–2 in the [[1986 FIFA World Cup Final|final]] at [[Mexico City]]'s [[Estadio Azteca]]. Argentina would not win the World Cup again until [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]], 36 years later. Total attendance was 2,394,031, an average per match of 46,039.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/mexico1986/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131220184547/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/mexico1986/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 December 2013 |title=1986 FIFA World Cup Mexico |work=FIFA.com |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |access-date=15 June 2014}}</ref> [[Canada men's national soccer team|Canada]], [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] and [[Iraq national football team|Iraq]] made their first appearances at the final stage.

The format of the competition changed from [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982]]. The final pair of matches in each group started at the same time<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.algeria.com/blog/the-game-that-changed-the-world-cup-algeria|title=The Game that Changed the World Cup&nbsp;— Algeria|work=algeria.com|access-date=15 September 2009|archive-date=10 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910220511/http://www.algeria.com/blog/the-game-that-changed-the-world-cup-algeria|url-status=live}}</ref> and the second round was played on a knockout basis rather than groups. The 24 teams qualified were divided into six groups of four (A to F). The top two teams and the four best third-place finishers from the six groups advanced to the knockout round of 16 teams. [[Italy national football team|Italy]] were the defending champions, but were eliminated by [[France national football team|France]] in the round of 16.

The tournament saw the appearance of the [[Mexican wave]], a spectator phenomenon which was popularised worldwide after featuring during the tournament.<ref>{{cite news |title=Who invented the Mexican Wave? |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/8742454.stm |access-date=12 September 2018 |agency=BBC |archive-date=12 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180912131612/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/8742454.stm |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://au.fourfourtwo.com/news/168505,fan-crazes.aspx |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110173626/http://au.fourfourtwo.com/news/168505%2Cfan-crazes.aspx |archive-date=10 November 2013 |title=...Fan Crazes |first=Andy |last=Jackson |date=11 June 2010 |publisher=[[FourFourTwo (Australia)]] |access-date=25 August 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/news-and-comment/the-100-greatest-world-cup-moments-1915569.html?action=Gallery&ino=94 |title=The 100 greatest World Cup moments. # 94. The Mexican Wave |first=Simon |last=Rice |newspaper=The Independent |date=10 June 2010 |access-date=25 August 2011 |archive-date=20 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181120072219/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/news-and-comment/the-100-greatest-world-cup-moments-1915569.html?action=Gallery&ino=94 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Host selection==
{{Main|FIFA World Cup hosts}}
{{See also|Colombian withdrawal from the 1986 FIFA World Cup}}
[[File:Exposición “¿De qué color pinta el verde? - El Colegio Nacional - 08.jpg|thumb|[[Adidas Azteca]] Football ball used in the 1986 World Cup in Mexico]]
[[Colombia]] was originally chosen as [[FIFA World Cup hosts#1986 FIFA World Cup|hosts]] by [[FIFA]] in June 1974, with [[Bogotá]], [[Medellín]], [[Cali]], [[Pereira, Colombia|Pereira]] and [[Bucaramanga]] intended as the host cities, plus potentially [[Barranquilla]].<ref name=WS>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[World Soccer (magazine)|World Soccer]]|title=1986 - World Cup without a home?|last=Maier|first=Hanns J|pages=24–25|date=June 1979}}</ref> Colombia agreed to host a 16-team competition. However, days before the [[1978 FIFA World Cup Group 2#West Germany vs Poland|1978 FIFA World Cup's opening match]], FIFA later allowed an expansion to 24 teams for the [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982 World Cup]] in [[Spain]], which was more challenging for Colombia to host, although FIFA President [[João Havelange]] initially gave assurances that they could revert to a 16-team tournament.<ref name=WS/> Colombian president [[Julio César Turbay Ayala]] was initially against holding the tournament in his country, but reluctantly granted permission in October 1980.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/98518653|title=for the record SOCCER|date=23 October 1980|website=Detroit Free Press|access-date=11 October 2021|archive-date=2 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211102194043/http://www.newspapers.com/image/98518653/|url-status=live}}</ref> However, the next Colombian president, [[Belisario Betancur]], declared on 5 November 1982 that they could not afford to host the World Cup under the terms that FIFA demanded.<ref>{{cite book|last=Pougatch|first=Mark|title=Three Lions Versus the World: England's World Cup Stories from the Men Who Were There|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jcLeQJan_skC&pg=PT175|date=11 January 2011|publisher=Mainstream Publishing|isbn=978-1-907195-59-4|page=175}}</ref>
[[File:Fuente de los Balones, en Querétaro.jpg|thumb|Football fountain created in 1986 to commemorate the FIFA World Cup in Mexico. [[Querétaro]] was one of the venues and this fountain is located just a few blocks away from the [[Estadio Corregidora|Corregidora Stadium]].]]
Although [[Mexico]], [[United States]] and [[Canada]] submitted bids on 11 March 1983 to be the replacement host, ([[Brazil]] was the only other eligible bidder but did not bid) the five-person special FIFA committee responsible for recommending the bids to the [[FIFA Executive Committee|Executive Committee]] (Exco) announced on 31 March that it would only consider Mexico's bid, saying the United States and Canada had "deviated" from FIFA's criteria<ref name="NYT 0508">{{cite news |title=A place for soccer's World Cup |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/08/sports/a-place-for-soccer-s-world-cup.html |date=8 May 1983 |first=Lawrie |last=Mifflin |newspaper=New York Times |access-date=8 October 2020 |archive-date=21 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210821230431/https://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/08/sports/a-place-for-soccer-s-world-cup.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and Exco members refused to visit Canadian and American stadium sites.<ref name="NYT 0521">{{cite news |title=Mexico Is Chosen As World Cup Host |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/21/sports/mexico-is-chosen-as-world-cup-host.html |date=21 May 1983 |publisher=UPI |access-date=8 October 2020 |archive-date=16 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210816021427/https://www.nytimes.com/1983/05/21/sports/mexico-is-chosen-as-world-cup-host.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 May, the committee announced Mexico as the replacement hosts despite Havelange stating that the United States and Canada had made better presentations.<ref name="NYT 0521"/> Mexico became the first nation to host two World Cups, as it had hosted the [[1970 FIFA World Cup]].

The Canadian representatives criticised the committee's decision not to consider Canada, saying they had submitted a more complete bid than Mexico, and that they had been misled by the number of stadiums required for bidding.<ref name="NYT 0521"/> The United States' bid contained more than the required number of stadiums with the required capacity to host World Cup matches (at least 40,000 capacity, 60,000 for second-round matches and 80,000 for the tournament final). Mexico submitted a bid with 14 stadiums, only six of which seated over 40,000 at the time of the bid, and three over 60,000.<ref name="NYT 0521"/> Havelange in turn criticised the United States' handling of stadium selection for the [[Football at the 1984 Summer Olympics|football tournament at the 1984 Summer Olympics in Los Angeles]]. The Americans also pointed out that Mexico had the influence of two seats on the 22-person executive committee, a FIFA vice president and an [[Guillermo Cañedo de la Bárcena|executive]] at [[Televisa]], a Mexican television network with ties to Havelange.<ref name="NYT 0508"/> Also working in Mexico's favour was Havelange secretly promising the broadcast rights to Televisa ahead of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/09/07/sports/soccer/07iht-soccer07.html |newspaper=New York Times |title=One of FIFA's Own Speaks Out |first=Rob |last=Hughes |date=7 September 2011 |access-date=8 October 2020 |archive-date=3 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210803171316/https://www.nytimes.com/2011/09/07/sports/soccer/07iht-soccer07.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Following the bidding process, [[Henry Kissinger]], the former [[United States Secretary of State]] who led the United States bid committee, remarked, "The politics of soccer make me nostalgic for the politics of the Middle East," while the leader of the Canadian committee called Mexico's 10-page bid document "a joke."<ref name="NYT 0521"/>

A [[1985 Mexico City earthquake|severe earthquake]] in September 1985, eight months before the tournament, cast doubt<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1985-12-15-sp-628-story.html|title=World Cup : Draw Comes as Mexico Tries to Pick Up Pieces|date=15 December 1985|website=Los Angeles Times|access-date=22 October 2020|archive-date=21 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240521013011/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1985-12-15-sp-628-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> over Mexico's ability to organise the event, but the stadiums were not affected and it was decided to go ahead with the preparations.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1985-09-23-mn-19523-story.html|title=EARTHQUAKES IN MEXICO : World Cup Still On, Officials Say|date=23 September 1985|website=Los Angeles Times|access-date=22 October 2020|archive-date=4 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201104030338/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1985-09-23-mn-19523-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> As 1986 had been declared the [[International Year of Peace]] by the [[United Nations]], the advertising boards of all the stadia displayed the [[FIFA]] and United Nations logos along with the legend "Football for Peace – Peace Year".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2012-05-04 |title=International Year of Peace 1986 Stamps |url=http://peace.gnet.no/ |access-date=2023-11-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120504170353/http://peace.gnet.no/ |archive-date=4 May 2012 }}</ref>

For the design of the logo an unofficial motto was adopted: "El Mundo Unido por Un Balón" ("The World United by a Ball").<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-09-03 |title=Creador del mejor logo de los mundiales: "Nadie me ha felicitado" |url=https://www.espn.com.ar/futbol/mundial/nota/_/id/6026941/creador-del-mejor-logo-de-los-mundiales-nadie-me-ha-felicitado |access-date=2022-11-26 |website=ESPN.com.ar |language=es |archive-date=26 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221126040310/https://www.espn.com.ar/futbol/mundial/nota/_/id/6026941/creador-del-mejor-logo-de-los-mundiales-nadie-me-ha-felicitado |url-status=live }}</ref>

The final draw took place on 15 December 1985 hosted at [[Televisa San Ángel]] studios in Mexico City.

==Qualification==
{{Main|1986 FIFA World Cup qualification}}
{{Original research section|date=May 2026}}
Three teams qualified for the World Cup for the first time: [[Canada men's national soccer team|Canada]], [[Denmark national football team|Denmark]] and [[Iraq national football team|Iraq]]. Iraq played all their 'home' matches on neutral ground in [[Saudi Arabia]] because of the [[Iran–Iraq War]]. Canada would not appear again until [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022]], and Iraq would not qualify again until the next time the tournament was held in Mexico in [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026]]. South Korea qualified for the first time since 1954, Paraguay for the first time since 1958, Portugal for the first time since 1966, Morocco for the first time since 1970 and Bulgaria and Uruguay for the first time since 1974. It remains as of 2026 the last appearance of Hungary and Northern Ireland.

All six previous World Cup-winning nations ([[Argentina national football team|Argentina]], [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]], [[England national football team|England]], [[Germany national football team|West Germany]], [[Italy national football team|Italy]], and [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]]) qualified, which held the record of most previous champions at a tournament before the record was broken in 2002 with the addition of [[France national football team|France]] (who won the 1998 tournament) and a second time in 2014 with the addition of [[Spain national football team|Spain]] (who won the 2010 tournament).

